  "Question": "How many intersection points does the parabola $y=x^2+2x+5$ have with the x-axis?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "2",
    "B": "1",
    "C": "0"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The discriminant of the equation $x^2+2x+5=0$ is $\\Delta=4-20=-16<0$. There are no real roots, so the parabola has no intersection points with the x-axis.\n\nAlternative method 1: Show the function is always positive\n\nAn x-axis intersection would require \\(y=0\\). Completing the square shows that this cannot happen:\n\\[\ny=x^2+2x+5=(x+1)^2+4\\ge4>0\n\\]\nfor every real \\(x\\), because a real square is nonnegative. Thus there are \\(0\\) intersection points, choice C.",
